A||||13||Tom88gt||radiator fan always on||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||10-28-2000||05:16 PM||||How do I test the relay or temp switch?  Which is most liely to be the culprit?  On Line Service Guide points to these two items but no testing guidance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-28-2000||05:34 PM||||Easy enough.  Pull the wire off the temp. switch.  If the fan stops, you know the switch has been keeping it on.  If not, it's the relay or wiring (e.g - a short in the wire that goes to the switch, etc.).  ||||reg||7
Z||000002||Tom88gt||10-28-2000||06:35 PM||||Can you help me ID the temp switch?  I've located 3 switches in the proximity of the thermostat.  One is just below and to the forward right of the thermostat housing with 3 wires.  Two others are just to the left of the thermostat and are mounted into the lower...intake manifold?  One has a single wire and the other one I think has two.<P>How many wires should this switch have?   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||10-28-2000||08:09 PM||||Found the temp switch.  Hidden under the EGR solenoid.  Disconnected it but no change.  The radiator fan keeps on going.  I pulled the relay up front and did a continuity check on it, appears normal.  The wiring to the relay, the small wire which operates the relay, had power.  What about the AC?  Where is the fan switch for the AC?||||reg||8
Z||000004||robertjay||10-29-2000||12:55 AM||||I have the factory service manual for my 85, and it shows the relay can be grounded by the A/C Pressure switch, the Coolant Temperature Switch, and the A/C Controls on the dash.  It appears to be the same for the V-6 or the 4 cylinder.  Hope this helps, if you want, I can scan the pictures of the book and e-mail you.  They are very good descriptions of functions and wires.  (Usually this manual is sort of difficult to decipher the info).  Later!<p>[This message has been edited by robertjay (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||10-29-2000||10:53 AM||||Hmmm...where might this A/C pressure switch be located?  I'm pretty sure the problem is one of the fan switches has failed, sending constant power to the relay.  The problem is finding these switches and knowing how to test them...||||reg||5
Z||000006||Tom88gt||10-29-2000||11:24 AM||||Well, I got under the car and pulled the wire off the red AC pressure switch on the back of the compressor and the fan magically quit :-)  I had replaced this switch a few months ago when it was leaking.  I had thought this was a high pressure cutoff switch to protect the AC system from over pressurization but I guess it serves another purpose...||||reg||7
Z||000007||MrPBody||10-29-2000||12:22 PM||||When I replaced the AC compressor and switches on my 4-cylinder, I found that the aftermarket switches were built "backwards" from the originals.  That is, the wiring harness connector for the high-pressure cutoff switch would only fit the fan switch, and vice versa.  Discovered this of course after I installed them and charged the system.  I'll fix it next year, when I need AC again; for now, I have unplugged the switch.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tom88gt||10-29-2000||05:15 PM||||I'm wondering why the AC high pressure switch is always grounded.  If my AC system is over filled, would that cause the switch to ground even when the AC is not running?  Thus causing the radiator fan to always run?  When I run the AC, the compressor cycles at about a 5-10 second interval which I think is too often.  ||||reg||5
Z||000009||MrPBody||10-29-2000||11:43 PM||||I could be wrong about this (it wouldn't be the first time), but I think the high-pressure cutoff switch is normally grounded and opens when refrigerant pressure rises too high.  The fan switch, on the other hand, should be normally open.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||robertjay||10-30-2000||08:28 AM||||My service manual shows the switch to be closed above 280 psi, grounding the fan relay.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tom88gt||10-30-2000||07:58 PM||||There is a second wire going into the back of the compressor.  The lower wire apparantly controls the radiator fan as the fan quit running when I pulled the wire.  The upper wire I assume goes to another switch...perhaps the "real" high pressure cut off switch.  When I replaced the lower switch, I asked the parts store for an "AC high pressure cut-off switch"  which apparantly fits either port.  So I'm guessing that I now have 2 high pressure switches installed, which are normaly grounded, thus causing the fan to run continuously.  Well, I'm off to the parts store to search for the elusive "Fan Switch".  Do you think I'm on the right track?    ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tom88gt||10-30-2000||09:01 PM||||Had to order the "high side low pressure switch".  The parts computer lists it as "white with a single wire".  The other low pressure switch they had was green but has 2 wires.  Does the white one sound familiar?||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||10-30-2000||10:45 PM||||Yep, sounds right to me.  Both compressor switches seem to be the same diameter.  I'm assuming that the compressor on your V6 is like my 4-cylinder.||||reg||1
